x86_64-windows  the two static locals - clang spells `?s@?1??main@@9@4UP@@A` with the guard `?$TSS0@?1??main@@9@4HA` and an `_Init_thread_abort` landing pad, where cxx1 keeps its own unique static symbol `main.s` and `main.s$guard`, as every static-local case here does (array-of-class-list-length.nonames says the same). Every other name agrees. x86_64-linux and arm64-darwin are compared on the Mac: this box's clang has no headers for those targets.
x86_64-linux  cxx1 emits the C1 and D1 forms of constructors and destructors defined inside their class where clang emits only the C2 a use asks for - the difference implicit-move.nonames records. Measured on the Windows box with clang -target x86_64-linux-gnu over cxx1's own headers, every other name agreeing (this box's names.sh cannot, its clang having no headers for that target).
arm64-darwin  Apple's libc++ tags its own initializer_list members with an ABI tag (begin`B9nqe220106`Ev and the like) that cxx1's <initializer_list> does not carry - a header difference; every name net of the tag agrees; the static local is cxx1's `main.sagg` against `_ZZ4mainE4sagg` and clang's `__cxa_guard_abort` pad is not written - both recorded in docs/CONFORMANCE.md.
